WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.140
While 19th-century London was a place&nbsp;
where dreams often met dead ends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:06.900
one boy's curiosity would light up the world.

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:09.360
We take electricity for granted today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:12.240
but have you ever wondered who&nbsp;
made these discoveries possible?

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:16.800
Michael Faraday came from the&nbsp;
slums and revolutionized physics.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:21.060
Born in 1791, Faraday grew up&nbsp;
in a poor neighborhood south&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:22.080
of the River Thames, the son of an&nbsp;
apprentice to the village blacksmith.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:26.220
Life was incredibly harshlargely&nbsp;
due to the Industrial Revolution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:29.760
which brought a massive influx&nbsp;
of people into the city, leading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:33.300
to severe overcrowding and abysmal sanitary&nbsp;
conditions. (referring to poop on the street)

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:37.860
Faraday recalled being given one loaf of&nbsp;
bread that had to last him for a week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:39.720
hardly enough to sustain him.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:44.700
But he received plenty of spi ritual sustenance&nbsp;
from his family’s small Christian sect.

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:49.500
Faraday learned to read and write at Sunday&nbsp;
school - he barely had a primary school education.

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:55.140
What he lacked in money and education, he&nbsp;
made up for with an insatiable curiosity.

00:00:55.140 --> 00:01:01.680
He started working as a bookbinder at the age of&nbsp;
13, binding books hour after hour, day after day.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:05.760
The only perk was his ability to read&nbsp;
every book he could get his hands on.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:09.180
The more he read, the more he&nbsp;
understood what he lacked in education.

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:13.560
He tried his best to speak well and made sure&nbsp;
his friends corrected him when he didn’t.

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:17.340
One of his favorite books emphasized&nbsp;
the importance of critical thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:21.240
and the crucial role of observation&nbsp;
in understanding the world around him.

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:25.920
The book deeply impacted Faraday,&nbsp;
teaching him to rely on careful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:28.620
observation during his groundbreaking experiments.

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:34.020
Growing up, he had heard about the discovery in&nbsp;
Italy by Alessandro Volta of the voltaic cell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:38.700
or battery, which, for the first time ever,&nbsp;
provided a continuous flow of electricity.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:42.180
Before that, devices like&nbsp;
tin foil-lined glass jars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:45.600
stored electricity but released&nbsp;
their charge in a single burst.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:50.160
The idea of a continuous stream of&nbsp;
electricity ignited a fire within Faraday.

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:53.340
How could he choose any career&nbsp;
other than one in science?

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:57.840
Faraday managed to snag a ticket to&nbsp;
attend a lecture by Sir Humphry Davy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:01.320
at the Royal Institution, where eminent&nbsp;
scientists presented their research.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:04.140
A customer at the bookshop&nbsp;
where he worked, William Dance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:07.980
had been impressed with Faraday’s passion&nbsp;
for science and offered him a free ticket.

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:13.560
Faraday was mesmerized by the lecture,&nbsp;
neatly recording every word in his notes.

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:17.700
He hoped to get a job at the esteemed&nbsp;
scientific academy, the Royal Society.

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:22.500
Only to later find out that when he wrote&nbsp;
to its president, Sir Joseph Banks, Banks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:26.820
or perhaps his secretary, had written on&nbsp;
the letter he sent: “No answer required.”

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:30.420
Faraday wondered if he was destined to&nbsp;
bind books for the rest of his life.&nbsp;

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:32.400
But then…he got a break.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:37.620
The bookshop customer who had given Faraday the&nbsp;
ticket to see Humphrey Davy suggested Davy hire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:42.120
Faraday when Davy was temporarily blinded&nbsp;
by an explosion in his lab and needed help.

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:43.980
This was just a temporary position.

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:49.740
The only permanent opening Davy had at the Royal&nbsp;
Institution was for a bottle washer..he had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:54.480
fire the one he had for fighting….it was the&nbsp;
lowest of low positions at the Institution.

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:56.160
But it was a foot in the door.

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:57.720
And Faraday accepted.

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:01.560
Davy recognized his talents, and&nbsp;
soon, he became a lab assistant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:04.980
helping with experiments like&nbsp;
extracting sugar from beetroot.

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:09.060
Working for Davy afforded him a unique&nbsp;
opportunity to travel across Europe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:12.540
mingle with leading scientists,&nbsp;
and open his eyes to the world.

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:16.860
Quite the journey for the son of a blacksmith&nbsp;
who had never ventured outside of London.

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:21.000
He built a reputation as a proficient&nbsp;
chemist, then the frontier of science.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:24.000
He analyzed the gases emitted by aging eggs.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.600
Tested methods of drying various&nbsp;
meats and fish as food for sailors.

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:30.720
And isolated the organic&nbsp;
chemical compound benzene.

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:36.120
Then came bombshell news that turned his&nbsp;
attention to another domain in science.

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:39.960
Hans Christian Oersted in Denmark&nbsp;
had put a magnetic compass near&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:43.440
his battery and was surprised to&nbsp;
see that the compass needle moved.

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:47.880
His discovery revealed that an electric&nbsp;
current could generate a magnetic field.

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:52.800
At that time, few scientists thought there was&nbsp;
any connection between electricity and magnetism.

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:57.120
The French physicist André-Marie&nbsp;
Ampère then described the intricate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:02.460
link between electricity and magnetism through&nbsp;
a detailed tapestry of mathematical formulas.

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:07.200
These math models were completely foreign&nbsp;
to Faraday because of his limited education.&nbsp;

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:09.240
It was akin to reading Chinese.

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:13.740
Yet, his lack of education may&nbsp;
have been a blessing in disguise.

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:17.280
Since Faraday couldn't lean&nbsp;
on formulas and equations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:20.880
he leaned into his intuition&nbsp;
and powers of observation.

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:24.780
He had to do his own experiments to&nbsp;
understand a topic which pushed him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:27.300
to design experiments that&nbsp;
others hadn't dreamed of.

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:32.160
One of his early experiments showed how&nbsp;
electricity and magnetism could produce motion.

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:35.040
He stuck a magnet at the&nbsp;
bottom of a glass container.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:37.320
Then filled the glass with a conductive liquid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:40.740
mercury in his case, water&nbsp;
with table salt in our case.

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:42.900
He dangled a wire over the cup.

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:48.180
When he passed an electric current through&nbsp;
the wire, the wire rotated around the magnet.

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:52.020
This rotational motion was due to the&nbsp;
interaction between the magnetic field&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:56.220
produced by the electric current in the&nbsp;
wire and the magnetic field of the magnet.

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:59.400
Faraday had built the&nbsp;
world’s first electric motor.

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:02.640
Fast forward to today, and the legacy of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:06.420
principle can be seen every time a&nbsp;
Tesla glides past you on the road.

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:12.120
EVs are descendants of Faraday's foundational&nbsp;
work of turning electrical energy into motion.

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:16.260
His friend and brother-in-law George&nbsp;
helped with the experiment and recalled:

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:21.540
“I shall never forget the enthusiasm expressed&nbsp;
in his face and the sparkling in his eyes.”

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:24.240
Faraday, on the other hand, wrote in his journal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:28.560
simply, “Very satisfactory, but&nbsp;
make a more sensible apparatus.”

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:32.820
He published his work in a scientific&nbsp;
journal, catapulting him to fame.

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:35.580
Much to the apparent jealousy of Sir Humphry Davy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:39.240
who was now being overshadowed&nbsp;
by his former bottle cleaner.

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:41.820
Faraday became a fellow of the Royal Society.

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:45.720
His Friday evening lectures at the&nbsp;
Royal Institution became hugely popular.

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:51.060
He also began to give Christmas lectures for kids&nbsp;
at a time when education for children was rare.

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:52.860
The tradition continues today.

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:56.640
Faraday emerged as England’s&nbsp;
leading lecturer on science.

00:05:56.640 --> 00:06:01.080
He was offered numerous opportunities in&nbsp;
consulting that would have made him very wealthy.

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:04.440
But, he chose the pursuit of&nbsp;
science over financial gain.

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:09.060
While his professional life took off,&nbsp;
his personal life blossomed, too.

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:12.480
He fell in love with George’s&nbsp;
sister, 19-year-old Sarah.

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:15.420
They were devoted to one&nbsp;
another their whole lives.

00:06:15.420 --> 00:06:20.580
Which was an about-face for someone who&nbsp;
was once pessimistic about love, writing:

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:25.680
What is the pest and plague of human life?
And what is the curse that often brings a wife?&nbsp;

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:26.820
‘tis love.

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:30.600
They had no children of their&nbsp;
own yet lived rich lives.

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:34.740
Sarah supported him in any way she&nbsp;
could, ensuring he ate nourishing food&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:38.760
and making sure he had time to relax&nbsp;
after hard work in his laboratory.

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:42.180
Ten years had passed since his&nbsp;
famous electric motor experiment.

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:46.680
Though he made other discoveries in&nbsp;
between, something was nagging at him.

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:51.120
If an electric current could produce a&nbsp;
magnetic field, could the reverse be true:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:54.000
could amagnetic field induce an electric current?

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:56.820
Many had tried, all had failed.

00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:58.620
Yet Faraday persisted.

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:03.060
His observations led him to a critical&nbsp;
insight: it wasn't just the presence of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:07.860
a magnetic field that could induce an electric&nbsp;
current, but the change in the magnetic field.

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:11.280
Electricity only forms when the magnet is moved.

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:15.420
When he moved a magnet inside a coil of&nbsp;
wire, he induced an electric current.

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:18.960
But when the magnet was stationary,&nbsp;
no current was observed.

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:24.240
This groundbreaking work demonstrated a&nbsp;
two-way relationship: while electric currents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:29.160
can produce magnetic fields, the converse&nbsp;
is also true: A changing magnetic field&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:34.380
leads to the creation of electric currents—a&nbsp;
principle known as electromagnetic induction.

00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:36.540
Consider Niagara Falls.

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:42.540
The falling water turns big turbines, which&nbsp;
then spin magnets around coils of wire. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:46.620
creates shifting magnetic fields in the&nbsp;
coils. And just as Faraday discovered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:49.920
these changing magnetic fields&nbsp;
generate electric currents.

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:54.060
From there, the electricity is transmitted&nbsp;
through power lines straight to your home.

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:56.700
While Faraday's experiments were groundbreaking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.700 --> 00:08:01.260
it took serious math skills, which he&nbsp;
lacked, to realize their true potential.

00:08:01.260 --> 00:08:03.120
Enter James Clerk Maxwell.

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:07.980
He took the findings of Faraday (and others)&nbsp;
and translated their pioneering work into a set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:12.420
of comprehensive mathematical formulas,&nbsp;
now recognized as Maxwell's Equations.

00:08:12.420 --> 00:08:17.580
These equations elegantly express how&nbsp;
electric and magnetic fields are intertwined,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:22.920
ultimately leading to the identification&nbsp;
of electromagnetic waves like radio waves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:25.560
microwaves, visible light, and X-rays.

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:29.940
This understanding later played a&nbsp;
pivotal role in the creation of radios,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:33.120
TVs, and, in due course, cellular communication.

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:35.760
So, Faraday's hands-on experiments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:41.220
combined with Maxwell's theoretical mastery, laid&nbsp;
the foundation for today's technological era.

00:08:42.180 --> 00:08:45.000
Faraday was deeply interested in understanding the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:49.380
fundamental nature of forces and finding&nbsp;
a unifying explanation for them all.

00:08:49.380 --> 00:08:55.140
His faith drove his deep curiosity, as he saw&nbsp;
the laws of nature as the handiwork of God,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.140 --> 00:08:58.080
and by studying them, he was studying the divine.

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:03.300
He speculated about the possible relationship&nbsp;
between electricity and gravity…to no avail.

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:08.640
Some of his peers thought his limited math skills&nbsp;
made him ill-equipped for advanced theorizing.

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:12.720
As the years passed, Faraday's&nbsp;
cognitive abilities began to decline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:17.520
making it challenging to produce work at the&nbsp;
same intensity that once brought him renown.&nbsp;

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:21.180
The strain on his physical and&nbsp;
mental well-being showed in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.180 --> 00:09:23.640
untamed whiteness of his hair as he grew older.

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:25.920
Nevertheless, he did what he could.

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:28.920
He served as scientific&nbsp;
advisor to the Trinity House,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:33.300
a historic institution responsible for&nbsp;
mariner safety around the British Isles.

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:38.820
During Faraday’s time, lighthouses served&nbsp;
as vital guides, quite literally signaling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:41.280
the difference between life and death for sailors.

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:46.620
Under his guidance, electric lighting began&nbsp;
to replace traditional oil and gas lamps.

00:09:46.620 --> 00:09:50.580
He was so committed to his work&nbsp;
that even at the age of 70,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.580 --> 00:09:54.180
he personally went to sea to test&nbsp;
the reliability of the new lights!

00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:58.620
In recognition of his profound&nbsp;
impact on science, Faraday was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.620 --> 00:10:00.660
offered a knighthood, which he turned down.

00:10:00.660 --> 00:10:04.560
He felt that he should remain&nbsp;
“plain Mr. Faraday to the end."

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:08.280
Faraday did, however, accept a gift&nbsp;
of a house from Queen Victoria,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.280 --> 00:10:12.420
spending the last years of his life&nbsp;
in a house at Hampton Court in London.

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:19.980
On August 25, 1867, Michael Faraday died&nbsp;
of natural causes. He was 75 years old.

00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:25.500
His gravestone in London's Highgate Cemetery&nbsp;
is modest, mirroring his lifelong humility,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:30.180
yet standing in stark contrast to&nbsp;
his immense contributions to science.

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:34.800
Despite his challenges with math, Faraday shined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:37.920
through his deep understanding and&nbsp;
intuition about the natural world.

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:41.820
Like Faraday, many of us face&nbsp;
hurdles in areas we wish to excel in.

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:46.860
If you’d like to improve your skills in math or&nbsp;
any STEM topic, I highly recommend Brilliant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

